.TH ROOMSRV 2
.SH NAME
roomsrv \- serve a room for spire(1), and mount a program that serves one
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "roomsrv.m";
roomsrv := load Roomsrv Roomsrv->PATH;

Ware: adt {
    key:   string;
    price: int;
    text:  string;
    gone:  int;
};

Room: adt {
    name:  string;
    ctl:   chan of (string, chan of string);
    wares: chan of chan of string;
    done:  chan of int;
};

init:       fn(): string;
room:       fn(name: string): ref Room;
serve:      fn(fd: ref Sys->FD, r: ref Room);
mountprog:  fn(prog: string, argv: list of string, mntpt: string,
                flags: int): string;
stock:      fn(path: string): (string, array of ref Ware, string);
wares:      fn(w: array of ref Ware): string;
ware:       fn(w: array of ref Ware, key: string): ref Ware;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Roomsrv
is the Styx side of a room in
.IR spire (1),
so that writing a room is writing what it stocks and what it will do,
and not writing a file server.
.PP
A room is four files:
.TP
.B name
one line, what the room calls itself
.TP
.B wares
zero or more lines of
.I "KEY PRICE TEXT..."
.TP
.B ctl
written to;
.BI "coins " N
and
.BI "buy " KEY
.TP
.B status
one line, the answer to the last write
.PP
.B Init
loads
.IR styx (2),
.IR styxservers (2)
and
.I nametree
and returns nil, or why not.
.B Room
makes a
.B Room
with its channels.
.PP
.B Serve
serves
.I r
on
.I fd
until the far end goes away, and runs until then, so a caller with
anything else to do spawns it.
A write to
.B ctl
arrives on
.BR r.ctl ,
with a channel to answer on: the answer is the line
.B status
is to hold, or, beginning with
.BR ! ,
the error the write is to fail with.
A refusal is an error on the write and not a line in
.BR status ,
so that a caller which ignores the error and reads
.B status
does not read the last thing that worked and believe it.
A read of
.B wares
arrives on
.BR r.wares ,
every time rather than once, because what a room has left may depend on
what has already been taken.
When the connection goes,
.B serve
sends on
.B r.done
if anything is listening, so a room can end rather than waiting on a
visitor who has already left.
.PP
.B Mountprog
runs a Dis program as a file server on a pipe and mounts what it serves
at
.IR mntpt ,
with
.I flags
as in
.IR sys-bind (2).
The program serves Styx on its standard input and output, which is what
.BI "mount {" prog "} " dir
and
.IR styxlisten (1)
already expect, so the same program works down a pipe, over a network,
or started by hand from a shell to look at.
It returns nil, or why not; nothing falls back to anything, because a
caller that could not mount has not got the thing and must say so.
.PP
The server is given a file descriptor group containing only its end of
the pipe and standard error, and a copy of the name space taken before
the mount is made.
Both matter.
A server that kept a copy of the whole descriptor table would still hold
the
.I client
end of its own pipe, so its read would never reach end of file and its
process would outlive every unmount; a server sharing the name space
that the mount lands in holds a reference to the mount of itself, with
the same result.
Getting either wrong leaves servers in
.IR ps (1)
after the program that started them has exited, and
.IR emu (1)
does not exit either.
.SS Stock
What a room stocks is kept in a file, so that a room's contents are data
like everything else.
.B Stock
reads one and returns the room's name, its wares, and an error.
The file is a header of
.BI "name " WORDS...
lines, then a line of
.BR % ,
then one ware a line as
.IR "KEY PRICE TEXT..." ;
lines beginning with
.B ;
are comments.
A price that is not a number is an error and says which line: Limbo's
.B "int \(dqabc\(dq"
is 0, so without that check a mistyped price would be a free ware and
nobody would be told.
.PP
.B Wares
renders what is left, one line each, skipping anything whose
.B gone
is set \- which is the text the
.B wares
file is to hold.
.B Ware
finds one by key.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/roomsrv.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR spire (1),
.IR styxservers (2),
.IR sys-bind (2),
.IR mntgen (4),
.IR styxlisten (1)
.SH BUGS
There is no authentication and none is wanted: a room is a game.
.B Mountprog
mounts what it is told to mount and a room is trusted with nothing,
because the game spends only what the room says it spent and understands
only three words from it.
.PP
Nothing times out.
A room that never answers a read leaves its visitor waiting.
